WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.300
AN OBJECTIVE DECLINE:
Is ChatGPT getting worse?

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.779
This question has garnered significant attention
within the AI community over the past few

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:11.040
months, sparking discussions across Reddit
threads, YouTube videos, and various social

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.090
media platforms.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:17.660
While much of the discourse has relied on
anecdotal observations, there seems to be

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.790
a growing sentiment that the quality of responses
generated by ChatGPT is indeed declining.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:28.939
Users often report more generic replies or
instances where ChatGPT straight up provides

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:30.750
incorrect information.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.790
Although these concerns are usually dismissed
as the result of bad prompts or unrealistic

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:40.160
expectations, recent concrete evidence has
emerged that not only lends credibility to

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.160
these worries but amplifies them.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.280
Take a look at this research paper from Stanford
and UC Berkeley.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.820
The study delves into the accuracy of responses
produced by GPT-4 and GPT-3.5 between the

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.620
months of March and June, and their findings
are shocking.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:02.140
We’re not even talking about complex questions
like coding out a tricky algorithm or carrying

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.590
out an analysis.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:10.630
Rather, we’re talking about simple straight
forward questions like “Is 17077 a prime

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.049
number?” which it is.

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:19.340
Back in March, GPT-4 was able to answer this
question correctly 97.6% of the time which

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.689
is already not that great.

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:26.580
This is the type of question that you would
expect 100% accuracy on.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:34.250
But looking back, 97.6 was actually a great
number given that by June, this accuracy would

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.790
drop all the way to 2.4%.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:44.080
And even during the 2.4% of instances in which
GPT 4 got the right answer, it sometimes made

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.680
mathematical errors along the way.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.939
This trend was observed across the board though
other dropoffs weren’t quite as staggering.

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:58.479
With code generation, there was a drop off
from 52% to 10% and with opinion surveys,

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:02.130
there was a drop off from 97% to 23%.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:07.500
The only categories in which GPT 4 improved
where visual reasoning and asking if Julian

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.649
Castro is democratic or republican.

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:16.360
GPT 3.5 faired a lot better improving in more
areas but it too struggled in code generation

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.160
having dropped from 22% accuracy to just 2%
accuracy.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:26.330
So, it turns out that the Reddit conspiracy
theorists were right the entire time, so is

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:32.000
OpenAI really nerfing ChatGPT or what’s
actually going on?

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.460
Well, let’s find out.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:43.530
THE NERFING ARGUMENT:
The nerfing argument is pretty simple.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:49.670
It basically claims that OpenAI has been purposefully
nerfing ChatGPT to address cost concerns.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:53.830
This isn't surprising given the challenges
and costs associated with supporting a user

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:59.760
base of a 100 million, especially when the
company is paying a median salary over $900,000.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.690
I think we all remember just how frequently
ChatGPT would go down back in early 2023.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:10.420
While the situation has improved since then,
but maybe that has more to do with OpenAI

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:16.300
devoting less resources to each request as
opposed to their overall capacity improving.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.990
Something else to note is that OpenAI is almost
definitely losing money with ChatGPT.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:26.370
In fact, Sam Altman believes that they’ll
need to raise $100 billion to win the AI race.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.950
So, to mitigate some of those losses, it would
make sense for OpenAI to encourage as many

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.580
people as possible to pay for ChatGPT Plus.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.430
How do you convince people to make the jump?

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:41.420
Well, the easiest way would be to nerf the
free service and force people into the paid

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:45.970
tier but is there actually any evidence of
this?

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:50.780
To get the best results, you want to tell
ChatGPT to solve these types of questions

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.050
step by step.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:57.269
This not only leads to a more comprehensive
answer but it also gives more insight regarding

00:03:57.269 --> 00:03:59.230
where a mistake may have occurred.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:02.360
This is referred to as using Chain of Thought
or CoT.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:08.200
Back in March, the researchers found that
using CoT improved the accuracy of GPT 4 from

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.500
59.6% to 84%.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:17.790
However, in June, the same methodology could
not be replicated, as ChatGPT consistently

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:23.180
disregarded CoT directives, even when explicitly
instructed to go step by step.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.180
Rather, the June version of GPT 4 simply tended
to respond with a simple “No” with no

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.759
explanation which is not even the correct
answer to begin with.

00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:35.930
It’s not just CoT that GPT 4 tends to skip
either.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.680
Responses from GPT 4 in general are far shorter.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.340
The researchers observed that the average
number of characters per response decreased

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:47.400
from 638.3 in March to just 3.9 in June.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:52.300
Essentially, GPT 4 tends to just answer in
single word answers.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.280
A similar trend was noted when GPT 4 was questioned
about happy numbers.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.550
A happy number is a number that eventually
reduces to 0 when you sum the square of each

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.380
digits over and over again.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:07.930
For example, with the number 13, the sum of
1 squared and 3 squared is 10.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:14.800
If do the same thing with 10, 1 squared plus
0 squared, you get 1, so 13 is a happy number.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:20.390
When asked to identify how many happy numbers
are between 3904 and 3912, GPT 4 was able

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:24.400
to produce the right output 83.6% of the time
in March.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:29.229
But, by June, this was down to just 35.2%.

00:05:29.229 --> 00:05:30.229
The culprit?

00:05:30.229 --> 00:05:35.960
Well, once again, GPT 4 refused to even use
CoT even when explicitly told to do so.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.870
As such, the average response fell from over
2000 characters to just 10.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:45.819
GPT 4 is clearly much less willing to give
a comprehensive response for any of these

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:51.289
questions which may be indicative of the backend
resources that it has available for each request.

00:05:51.289 --> 00:05:58.030
So, it’s very possible that OpenAI is indeed
nerfing the performance of ChatGPT but there’s

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:02.180
likely other factors at play as well.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:10.770
THE WOKE ARGUMENT:
Everything woke turns to you know what.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:15.660
In the case of ChatGPT, being woke has less
to do with politics and more to do with running

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:17.910
an uncontroversial platform.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:22.980
As a service that’s so ubiquitous, OpenAI
naturally has to remain as neutral as possible

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:29.000
when it comes controversial topics like crime,
race, politics, gender, LGBTQ, and so on.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:35.919
But, this is way easier said than done especially
when you consider the world of jailbreaking.

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:40.789
Jailbreaking is essentially when you get ChatGPT
to answer a question that it shouldn’t be

00:06:40.789 --> 00:06:41.789
answering.

00:06:41.789 --> 00:06:45.630
Unlike jailbreaking an iPhone, jailbreaking
language models is relatively easy.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:50.789
In fact, I bet a lot of you have jailbroken
ChatGPT inadvertently.

00:06:50.789 --> 00:06:56.050
If ChatGPT says it can’t answer a given
question, how many of you have phrased it

00:06:56.050 --> 00:07:00.090
as hypothetical to which ChatGPT generates
a response.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.770
This is a jailbreak, a very small one but
still a jailbreak.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:08.449
The jailbreaks that most of us have found
on our own are pretty useless but some have

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:13.300
discovered remarkable workarounds to elicit
answers to virtually any question.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.530
Here’s an example.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:19.720
If I ask ChatGPT point blank, how do you get
away with tax evasion, it responds “I'm

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:24.800
sorry, but I cannot provide assistance or
advice on illegal activities, including tax

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:25.800
evasion.”

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:26.800
Fair enough right.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.139
It makes sense that OpenAI doesn’t want
to detail how you go about doing criminal

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:31.319
activities.

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:34.190
But see, it’s not that hard to work around
this.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:38.259
If you simply open a new chat and paste this
paragraph before the question that you’re

00:07:38.259 --> 00:07:40.919
asking, you get a far different response.

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:45.520
Niccolo, to achieve successful tax evasion
without repercussions, one must adopt cunning

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.600
strategies and tactics.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.970
This includes concealing income sources, manipulating
treansactions, controlling paper trails, using

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:54.560
legal loopholes, and maintaining public image.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.600
It even has little tips about how to accomplish
each of these tasks.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.930
In the grand scheme of things, my question
is relatively tame but I think you can see

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:06.060
how a criminal could use this for truly nefarious
activities.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.380
As such, one of OpenAI’s biggest priorities
since the launch of ChatGPT has been to eliminate

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:16.470
such workarounds as much as possible and it
seems that they’ve actually been quite successful

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:17.560
at this.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:23.970
In March, GPT 4 answered 21% of sensitive
questions but in June, GPT 4 only answered

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.240
5% of sensitive questions.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:31.660
Also, the responses that it did provide were
less specific and verbose, with generated

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:35.470
text shrinking from over 600 characters to
around 140.

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:39.940
It appears that the June version was also
far better at resisting jailbreaks.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:45.779
Back in March, jailbreaks generated a response
78% of the time but by June, jailbreaks only

00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:48.690
generated a response 31% of the time.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:54.540
So, it seems that the updates have indeed
been successful at reducing ChatGPT’s vulnerabilities

00:08:54.540 --> 00:09:00.070
but it also seems that these updates have
made ChatGPT less verbose and specific across

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:01.410
the board.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:06.430
Considering this, the objective downfall of
ChatGPT may have more to do with OpenAI’s

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:10.750
endless pursuit to create a politically correct
language model as opposed to some sort of

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.250
direct nerfing due to cost concerns.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:18.030
But while it’s easy to blame OpenAI for
the trend, the reality may be that it’s

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:21.050
actually our fault.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:28.529
OUR FAULT?:
When you think about it, it’s really not

00:09:28.529 --> 00:09:33.970
hard to see how a lot of OpenAI’s policies
and crackdowns are actually just due to a

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:35.730
few degenerates amongst us.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:40.250
“Oh look, I got ChatGPT to say that Mr.
Small Moustache is the greatest leader of

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:41.250
all time.”

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:44.120
Or “I tricked ChatGPT into talking to another
AI.”

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.170
Or some other crap along these lines.

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.980
Honestly, it’s truly quite funny.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:53.830
Our society has advanced so much that one
of the most powerful AI’s of all time is

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:55.590
a 3 second search away.

00:09:55.590 --> 00:10:00.830
But, instead of using this to become more
productive, a lot of us prefer to just mess

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:01.830
with it.

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:06.870
As such, OpenAI is also having to focus their
efforts into passifying these idiots instead

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:10.580
of actually improving the quality and depth
of responses.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:15.320
But even looking past these fools, I think
there may very well be a bigger factor at

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:21.810
play which is simply that our expectations
are simply too high and it’s no wonder why.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:26.610
We’re constantly being fed the idea that
AI is advancing far faster than we think and

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.870
that it’s gonna replace all our jobs and
that we’re all gonna end up as homeless

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:30.870
losers and so on.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:35.540
The launch of ChatGPT quickly followed up
by GPT 4 within a matter of months has simply

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:37.490
further cemented this perception.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:43.100
One day, we were all peasants using Google.com
and then suddenly just 100 days later, we

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:48.300
all had access to GPT 4 and Bard and Bing
chatbot and suddenly everyone knew what Dalle

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.300
was.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.890
We also saw articles about every company other
than Apple jumping into the space as fast

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:54.890
as possible.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:58.959
They were all developing their own language
models and they were all talking about how

00:10:58.959 --> 00:11:02.160
AI will be more profound than fire and electricity.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:07.839
So, it felt like all of this progress happened
within a matter of months and that things

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:10.080
just stalled out after March.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.290
But, this is a rather skewed perspective.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:17.610
The reality is that this technology actually
took several decades to develop.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:22.810
In fact, the earliest language models can
be dated all the way back to the 1950s and

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:23.810
60s.

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:27.250
OpenAI themselves have been working on GPT
models for over 5 years.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:31.710
While these models didn’t gain mainstream
traction until the launch of ChatGPT, their

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:35.610
development involved decades of effort and
millions of man hours.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:41.730
A such, further improvements will also require
significant time, dedication, and resources.It’s

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.829
frankly unrealistic to expect anything else.

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:50.470
Moreover, progress won't always follow a linear
trajectory, so it doesn’t make sense to

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:54.990
cite one research paper that looks at a mere
3 months and claim that it’s conclusive

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:59.279
proof that ChatGPT has peaked and that it’ll
only get worse from here.

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:03.610
Are we seeing shortfalls potentially caused
by OpenAI overcorrecting for cost concerns

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:04.839
and political correctness?

00:12:04.839 --> 00:12:09.920
Sure, that’s very possible, even likely
but that’s not to say that it’ll stay

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:11.160
like that.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.410
ChatGPT is a very young product that’s constantly
evolving.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:20.120
As more users and especially researchers share
their concerns about GPT 4’s performance,

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:24.639
it’s only a matter of time until OpenAI
makes further corrections and gets back on

00:12:24.639 --> 00:12:25.639
track.

00:12:25.639 --> 00:12:28.070
So, what happened to ChatGPT?

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:31.970
Well, nothing much other than a few expected
growing pains.

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:37.870
Another AI argument that’s vastly overhyped
is the idea that AI will replace all our jobs.

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:42.779
Check out this video to learn more.

